    "['set protocols ospf area [area_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent for the given Juniper OSPF configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n passive-interface Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco requires an OSPF process ID (I used 1 as it's common)\\n2. The loopback interface is made passive separately in Cisco\\n3. The network statements use wildcard masks instead of CIDR notation\\n4. Cisco automatically makes loopbacks passive by default in newer IOS versions, but it's good practice to explicitly configure it\", 'trans_template': 'router ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent for the given Juniper OSPF configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n passive-interface Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco requires an OSPF process ID (I used 1 as it's common)\\n2. The loopback interface is made passive separately in Cisco\\n3. The network statements use wildcard masks instead of CIDR notation\\n4. Cisco automatically makes loopbacks passive by default in newer IOS versions, but it's good practice to explicitly configure it\", 'trans_template': 'network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent for the given Juniper OSPF configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n passive-interface Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco requires an OSPF process ID (I used 1 as it's common)\\n2. The loopback interface is made passive separately in Cisco\\n3. The network statements use wildcard masks instead of CIDR notation\\n4. Cisco automatically makes loopbacks passive by default in newer IOS versions, but it's good practice to explicitly configure it\", 'trans_template': 'network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'passive-interface Loopback0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'passive-interface Loopback0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent for the given Juniper OSPF configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n passive-interface Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco requires an OSPF process ID (I used 1 as it's common)\\n2. The loopback interface is made passive separately in Cisco\\n3. The network statements use wildcard masks instead of CIDR notation\\n4. Cisco automatically makes loopbacks passive by default in newer IOS versions, but it's good practice to explicitly configure it\", 'trans_template': 'passive-interface Loopback0'}]]": 1,
    "['interface [interface_name] passive', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper OSPF configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n passive-interface loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. In Cisco, you first enter OSPF configuration mode (typically using process ID 1 as a default)\\n2. The \"passive-interface\" command is used instead of Juniper\\'s \"passive\" flag under the interface\\n3. The loopback interface is specified by name (loopback0) rather than unit number (lo0.0)', 'trans_template': 'router ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'passive-interface loopback0', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper OSPF configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n passive-interface loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. In Cisco, you first enter OSPF configuration mode (typically using process ID 1 as a default)\\n2. The \"passive-interface\" command is used instead of Juniper\\'s \"passive\" flag under the interface\\n3. The loopback interface is specified by name (loopback0) rather than unit number (lo0.0)', 'trans_template': 'passive-interface loopback0'}]]": 1,
    "['interface [interface_name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet2/0/0', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS commands:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet2/0/0\\n mpls ip\\n ip ospf 1 area 0\\n##\\n \\nExplanation:\\n1. `set protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0.0` translates to `mpls ip` under the interface configuration in Cisco IOS\\n2. `set protocols ospf area 0.0.0.0 interface ge-2/0/0.0` translates to `ip ospf 1 area 0` under the interface configuration in Cisco IOS (assuming OSPF process ID 1)\\n\\nThe commands are combined under a single interface configuration context in Cisco IOS.\", 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet2/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ip', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS commands:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet2/0/0\\n mpls ip\\n ip ospf 1 area 0\\n##\\n \\nExplanation:\\n1. `set protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0.0` translates to `mpls ip` under the interface configuration in Cisco IOS\\n2. `set protocols ospf area 0.0.0.0 interface ge-2/0/0.0` translates to `ip ospf 1 area 0` under the interface configuration in Cisco IOS (assuming OSPF process ID 1)\\n\\nThe commands are combined under a single interface configuration context in Cisco IOS.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls ip'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ip ospf 1 area 0', 'parent_command': ['router ospf 1'], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS commands:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet2/0/0\\n mpls ip\\n ip ospf 1 area 0\\n##\\n \\nExplanation:\\n1. `set protocols mpls interface ge-2/0/0.0` translates to `mpls ip` under the interface configuration in Cisco IOS\\n2. `set protocols ospf area 0.0.0.0 inte